WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:04.856 --> 00:07.536 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference January 29,

00:07.696 --> 00:09.406 align:middle
2020 CHAIR POWELL.

00:09.536 --> 00:10.586 align:middle
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:10.846 --> 00:11.556 align:middle
Thanks for being here.

00:12.276 --> 00:16.666 align:middle
At today's meeting, my
colleagues and I decided

00:16.666 --> 00:18.506 align:middle
to leave our policy
rate unchanged.

00:19.586 --> 00:23.006 align:middle
As always, we base our decisions
on our judgment of how best

00:23.006 --> 00:25.136 align:middle
to achieve the goals
Congress has given us:

00:25.646 --> 00:27.636 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

00:29.116 --> 00:31.186 align:middle
We believe monetary
policy is well positioned

00:31.186 --> 00:32.476 align:middle
to serve the American people

00:32.806 --> 00:36.136 align:middle
by supporting continued economic
growth, a strong job market,

00:36.646 --> 00:39.316 align:middle
and a return of inflation to
our symmetric 2 percent goal.

00:40.136 --> 00:43.956 align:middle
The expansion is in its 11th
year, the longest on record.

00:44.486 --> 00:46.916 align:middle
Growth in household spending
moderated toward the end

00:46.916 --> 00:50.886 align:middle
of last year, but with a healthy
job market, rising incomes,

00:51.046 --> 00:52.696 align:middle
and upbeat consumer confidence,

00:53.226 --> 00:55.926 align:middle
the fundamentals supporting
household spending are solid.

00:57.356 --> 01:00.476 align:middle
In contrast, business investment
and exports remain weak,

01:01.116 --> 01:04.086 align:middle
and manufacturing output has
declined over the past year.

01:05.276 --> 01:08.196 align:middle
Sluggish growth abroad and trade
developments have been weighing

01:08.196 --> 01:09.656 align:middle
on activity in these sectors.

01:10.236 --> 01:12.576 align:middle
However, some of
the uncertainties

01:12.576 --> 01:14.076 align:middle
around trade have
diminished recently,

01:14.326 --> 01:17.506 align:middle
and there are some signs that
global growth may be stabilizing

01:17.646 --> 01:20.076 align:middle
after declining since mid-2018.

01:21.526 --> 01:23.906 align:middle
Nonetheless, uncertainties
about the outlook remain,

01:24.216 --> 01:26.816 align:middle
including those posed
by the new coronavirus.

01:28.866 --> 01:31.336 align:middle
Overall, with monetary and
financial conditions supportive,

01:31.336 --> 01:33.816 align:middle
we expect moderate
economic growth to continue.

01:34.416 --> 01:38.826 align:middle
The unemployment rate has
been near half-century lows

01:38.826 --> 01:41.076 align:middle
for well more than
a year, and the pace

01:41.076 --> 01:42.596 align:middle
of job gains remains solid.

01:44.136 --> 01:46.196 align:middle
Participation in the
labor force by people

01:46.196 --> 01:49.686 align:middle
in their prime working
years, ages 25 to 54,

01:49.876 --> 01:52.046 align:middle
is at its highest level
in more than a decade.

01:52.426 --> 01:54.596 align:middle
And wages have been
rising, particularly

01:54.596 --> 01:55.906 align:middle
for lower-paying jobs.

01:56.836 --> 01:59.136 align:middle
People who live and work in
middle-income communities

01:59.136 --> 02:01.576 align:middle
and low-income communities tell
us that many who have struggled

02:01.576 --> 02:04.016 align:middle
to find work are now
finding new opportunities.

02:05.256 --> 02:08.126 align:middle
Employment gains have been
broad based across all racial

02:08.126 --> 02:10.426 align:middle
and ethnic groups and
all levels of education.

02:10.946 --> 02:14.576 align:middle
These developments underscore
for us the importance

02:14.576 --> 02:15.926 align:middle
of sustaining the expansion

02:15.926 --> 02:18.746 align:middle
so that the strong job
market reaches more

02:18.746 --> 02:19.676 align:middle
of those left behind.

02:21.696 --> 02:24.636 align:middle
Inflation continues to
run below our symmetric 2

02:24.636 --> 02:25.406 align:middle
percent objective.

02:26.626 --> 02:28.246 align:middle
Over the 12 months
through November,

02:28.856 --> 02:33.226 align:middle
total PCE inflation was 1.5
percent, and core inflation,

02:33.226 --> 02:36.906 align:middle
which excludes volatile food and
energy prices, was 1.6 percent.

02:38.576 --> 02:41.926 align:middle
Available data suggest similar
inflation readings for December,

02:42.166 --> 02:44.606 align:middle
though we expect inflation
to move closer to 2 percent

02:44.606 --> 02:47.706 align:middle
over the next few months
as unusually low readings

02:47.706 --> 02:50.986 align:middle
from early 2019 drop
out of the calculation.

02:52.746 --> 02:55.606 align:middle
While low and stable inflation
is certainly a good thing,

02:56.126 --> 02:59.196 align:middle
inflation that runs persistently
below our objective can lead

02:59.276 --> 03:02.096 align:middle
longer-term inflation
expectations to drift down,

03:02.656 --> 03:04.556 align:middle
pulling actual inflation
even lower.

03:04.846 --> 03:08.306 align:middle
In turn, interest rates
would be lower as well-closer

03:08.436 --> 03:09.706 align:middle
to their effective lower bound.

03:10.186 --> 03:13.006 align:middle
As a result, we would have less
room to reduce interest rates

03:13.006 --> 03:16.746 align:middle
to support the economy in a
future downturn to the detriment

03:16.746 --> 03:18.356 align:middle
of American families
and businesses.

03:19.446 --> 03:21.876 align:middle
We have seen this dynamic
play out in other economies

03:21.876 --> 03:23.646 align:middle
around the world,
and we are determined

03:23.646 --> 03:25.246 align:middle
to avoid it here in
the United States.

03:25.476 --> 03:28.876 align:middle
In particular, we
believe the current stance

03:28.876 --> 03:30.616 align:middle
of monetary policy
is appropriate

03:30.616 --> 03:34.216 align:middle
to support sustained economic
growth, a strong labor market,

03:34.216 --> 03:37.746 align:middle
and inflation returning to
our 2 percent-symmetric 2

03:37.746 --> 03:38.456 align:middle
percent objective.

03:39.716 --> 03:41.156 align:middle
As long as incoming information

03:41.156 --> 03:43.196 align:middle
about the economy
remains broadly consistent

03:43.196 --> 03:45.216 align:middle
with this outlook,
the current stance

03:45.216 --> 03:47.966 align:middle
of monetary policy likely
will remain appropriate.

03:48.086 --> 03:50.566 align:middle
We will be monitoring
the effects

03:50.566 --> 03:52.506 align:middle
of the policy actions
we took last year,

03:52.726 --> 03:55.076 align:middle
along with other information
bearing on the outlook,

03:55.916 --> 03:58.256 align:middle
as we assess the appropriate
path of the target range

03:58.256 --> 03:59.246 align:middle
for the federal funds rate.

04:00.226 --> 04:01.656 align:middle
Of course, if developments
emerge

04:01.656 --> 04:03.856 align:middle
that cause a material
reassessment of our outlook,

04:04.546 --> 04:05.896 align:middle
we would respond accordingly.

04:06.416 --> 04:08.166 align:middle
Policy is not on
a preset course.

04:08.446 --> 04:13.716 align:middle
I will conclude with a brief
overview of our current plans

04:13.716 --> 04:16.766 align:middle
for our technical operations
to implement monetary policy.

04:17.246 --> 04:21.056 align:middle
The plan the FOMC
announced back in October

04:21.056 --> 04:22.396 align:middle
to purchase Treasury bills

04:22.396 --> 04:25.076 align:middle
and conduct repo operations
has proceeded smoothly

04:25.606 --> 04:28.366 align:middle
and has succeeded in providing
an ample supply of reserves

04:28.366 --> 04:29.326 align:middle
to the banking system

04:29.796 --> 04:31.846 align:middle
and effectively controlling
the federal funds rate.

04:32.846 --> 04:34.456 align:middle
In light of the resulting
stability

04:34.456 --> 04:35.406 align:middle
in the federal funds rate

04:35.406 --> 04:37.406 align:middle
and money market
conditions more generally,

04:37.916 --> 04:40.606 align:middle
we decided to make a small
technical upward adjustment

04:40.656 --> 04:42.286 align:middle
to administered rates to ensure

04:42.286 --> 04:44.356 align:middle
that the federal
funds rate trades well

04:44.356 --> 04:45.436 align:middle
within the target range.

04:46.416 --> 04:49.016 align:middle
This action reverses the
small downward adjustment made

04:49.016 --> 04:51.416 align:middle
in September when money
markets were volatile.

04:53.486 --> 04:56.236 align:middle
As our bill purchases continue
to build reserves toward levels

04:56.236 --> 04:58.946 align:middle
that maintain ample
conditions, the role played

04:58.946 --> 05:01.786 align:middle
by active repo operations
will naturally recede.

05:02.866 --> 05:04.186 align:middle
Over the first half
of this year,

05:04.436 --> 05:07.896 align:middle
we intend to adjust the size
and pricing of repo operations

05:07.896 --> 05:10.856 align:middle
as we transition away
from their active use

05:10.916 --> 05:12.166 align:middle
in supplying reserves.

05:13.236 --> 05:17.036 align:middle
This process will take place
gradually, and, as indicated

05:17.036 --> 05:19.346 align:middle
in today's FOMC directive
to the Desk,

05:19.806 --> 05:22.796 align:middle
we expect to continue offering
repos at least through April

05:23.026 --> 05:25.706 align:middle
to ensure a consistently
ample supply of reserves.

05:26.966 --> 05:29.796 align:middle
Based on current projections, we
expect that the underlying level

05:29.796 --> 05:33.206 align:middle
of reserves will durably
reach ample levels sometime

05:33.206 --> 05:34.646 align:middle
in the second quarter
of this year.

05:35.206 --> 05:38.646 align:middle
As we get close to that point,
we intend to slow the pace

05:38.646 --> 05:40.866 align:middle
of purchases and
transition to a program

05:40.866 --> 05:42.986 align:middle
of smaller reserve
management purchases

05:43.406 --> 05:45.496 align:middle
that maintains an
ample level of reserves

05:45.496 --> 05:47.296 align:middle
without the active use of repos.

05:47.816 --> 05:51.386 align:middle
At that point, as in
the pre-crisis period,

05:51.796 --> 05:54.696 align:middle
our balance sheet will be
expanding gradually over time,

05:55.136 --> 05:57.756 align:middle
reflecting the trend growth
in the demand for currency

05:57.756 --> 05:59.466 align:middle
and other Federal
Reserve liabilities.

06:00.946 --> 06:02.746 align:middle
All of these technical
measures are designed

06:02.746 --> 06:05.416 align:middle
to support the efficient
and effective implementation

06:05.416 --> 06:07.786 align:middle
of monetary policy
and are not intended

06:07.786 --> 06:09.576 align:middle
to represent a chance-a change

06:09.576 --> 06:10.956 align:middle
in the stance of
monetary policy.

06:11.346 --> 06:13.566 align:middle
We are committed to
completing the transition

06:13.566 --> 06:17.836 align:middle
to our longer-run ample-reserves
regime smoothly and predictably.

06:18.216 --> 06:20.996 align:middle
Of course, we will continue
to closely monitor conditions

06:20.996 --> 06:23.896 align:middle
in money markets, and we
will adjust these plans

06:24.466 --> 06:25.626 align:middle
as conditions warrant.

06:26.516 --> 06:28.446 align:middle
Thank you, and I'll be happy
to take your questions.

06:32.386 --> 06:33.816 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER CONDON.

06:34.076 --> 06:34.536 align:middle
Thank you.

06:34.536 --> 06:36.256 align:middle
Chris Condon, Bloomberg News.

06:36.736 --> 06:39.316 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, I would
like you to comment

06:39.316 --> 06:40.426 align:middle
in a little bit more depth

06:40.426 --> 06:42.806 align:middle
about one small change I've
noted in the statement.

06:44.216 --> 06:47.486 align:middle
It notes that policy
will be appropriate

06:47.596 --> 06:51.066 align:middle
to bring-the Committee
believes-inflation back

06:51.146 --> 06:55.206 align:middle
to the Committee's 2 percent
symmetric inflation objective.

06:55.206 --> 06:57.246 align:middle
That's a slight change
from the last time,

06:57.806 --> 06:59.166 align:middle
when you were expecting it

06:59.166 --> 07:04.186 align:middle
to bring inflation outcomes
back near the objective.

07:04.786 --> 07:07.626 align:middle
And I would put this also in the
context of a comment you made

07:07.916 --> 07:10.376 align:middle
at the last press conference
where you drew attention

07:10.376 --> 07:11.746 align:middle
to the fact that a number

07:11.746 --> 07:17.776 align:middle
of policymakers had projected
inflation overshoots two

07:17.776 --> 07:21.186 align:middle
or three years out under
appropriate monetary policy.

07:21.326 --> 07:24.396 align:middle
Should we take all of this
together to mean simply

07:24.766 --> 07:26.596 align:middle
that the Committee
is more confident

07:27.326 --> 07:31.176 align:middle
that a 2 percent outcome for
inflation is already baked

07:31.276 --> 07:35.136 align:middle
in the cake, or that
this is a signal

07:35.496 --> 07:38.166 align:middle
that the Committee has
a stronger resolution

07:38.166 --> 07:43.286 align:middle
to bring inflation at least
to the 2 percent objective

07:43.846 --> 07:49.436 align:middle
and put-bring into play an
informal makeup strategy

07:49.506 --> 07:50.076 align:middle
for inflation?

07:50.236 --> 07:50.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:50.826 --> 07:56.056 align:middle
Yes. So, in making that change,
our goal was, really-that was,

07:56.056 --> 07:58.846 align:middle
changing "near" to
"returning to"-was

07:58.846 --> 08:00.906 align:middle
to avoid possible
misinterpretation.

08:01.446 --> 08:04.016 align:middle
So you may remember, in the
December minutes we noted

08:04.016 --> 08:06.766 align:middle
that a few Committee members
suggested that the language

08:06.766 --> 08:10.186 align:middle
that stated that monetary policy
would support inflation near 2

08:10.186 --> 08:12.876 align:middle
percent could be
misinterpreted as suggesting

08:12.876 --> 08:14.376 align:middle
that policymakers
were comfortable

08:14.376 --> 08:16.196 align:middle
with inflation running
below that level.

08:17.006 --> 08:20.416 align:middle
So we thought about that
in the intermeeting period

08:20.416 --> 08:23.626 align:middle
and concluded that it would
be appropriate to adjust

08:23.626 --> 08:25.656 align:middle
that language to
send a clearer signal

08:25.656 --> 08:26.576 align:middle
that we're not comfortable

08:26.576 --> 08:29.656 align:middle
with inflation rising
persistently-running

08:29.656 --> 08:31.986 align:middle
persistently below our 2
percent symmetric objective.

08:32.546 --> 08:34.126 align:middle
So, yes, there is
something in that.

08:34.646 --> 08:37.526 align:middle
It's just that we wanted to
underscore our commitment

08:38.046 --> 08:39.886 align:middle
to 2 percent not being a ceiling

08:39.916 --> 08:42.176 align:middle
to inflation running
around-symmetrically

08:42.176 --> 08:45.196 align:middle
around 2 percent, and
that we're not satisfied

08:45.196 --> 08:48.386 align:middle
with inflation running below 2
percent, particularly at a time

08:48.476 --> 08:52.166 align:middle
such as now where we're a
long way into an expansion

08:52.166 --> 08:54.826 align:middle
and a long way into a period
of very low unemployment when,

08:54.826 --> 08:57.446 align:middle
in theory, inflation
should be moving up.

08:59.666 --> 09:05.146 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

09:05.316 --> 09:05.586 align:middle
Thank you.

09:05.586 --> 09:07.166 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

09:08.026 --> 09:10.296 align:middle
I want to ask about
the balance sheet.

09:10.706 --> 09:12.376 align:middle
How many reserves are "ample"?

09:12.376 --> 09:16.446 align:middle
How many reserves does the
Fed now think it will need

09:16.496 --> 09:18.656 align:middle
to conduct policy in
its current framework?

09:18.656 --> 09:19.946 align:middle
And can you walk
me through how you

09:19.946 --> 09:21.896 align:middle
and your colleagues are
arriving at that answer?

09:21.896 --> 09:22.546 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:22.766 --> 09:25.606 align:middle
Sure. So, thanks
for that question.

09:25.606 --> 09:29.156 align:middle
Why don't I say a few
things about repos,

09:29.156 --> 09:30.366 align:middle
since I know that'll
be of interest.

09:30.366 --> 09:32.146 align:middle
So, just to bring you back,

09:32.146 --> 09:35.036 align:middle
after last September's brief
turmoil, we took prompt

09:35.036 --> 09:37.216 align:middle
and decisive action, and
a result-as a result,

09:37.916 --> 09:39.916 align:middle
money markets have been
operating smoothly since then.

09:40.456 --> 09:42.926 align:middle
Just to review, those two
adjustments were Treasury bill

09:42.926 --> 09:46.036 align:middle
purchases of at least-of
$60 billion-sorry-at least

09:46.036 --> 09:48.756 align:middle
into the second quarter,
and term and overnight repo

09:48.756 --> 09:49.616 align:middle
at least through April.

09:49.676 --> 09:52.086 align:middle
And I will get to your
specific question.

09:52.086 --> 09:54.216 align:middle
The purpose of the
adjustments has been to assure

09:54.216 --> 09:56.146 align:middle
that our policy is
transmitted smoothly

09:56.146 --> 09:57.186 align:middle
to the federal funds rate,

09:57.656 --> 10:00.406 align:middle
which requires well-functioning
money markets.

10:00.746 --> 10:01.546 align:middle
It doesn't mean we're trying

10:01.546 --> 10:04.256 align:middle
to eliminate all volatility
in the repo markets.

10:04.256 --> 10:06.726 align:middle
We know that some volatility
is normal and expected

10:06.836 --> 10:08.206 align:middle
in well-functioning markets.

10:08.646 --> 10:11.226 align:middle
And, as I mentioned, these
adjustments have been successful

10:11.266 --> 10:13.256 align:middle
in supplying an ample
quantity of reserves.

10:13.586 --> 10:15.896 align:middle
Money markets operated
smoothly, including right

10:15.896 --> 10:18.626 align:middle
through year-end, and the
fed funds rate has remained

10:18.626 --> 10:19.386 align:middle
in our target range.

10:20.176 --> 10:21.376 align:middle
So we know-we will know

10:21.376 --> 10:23.276 align:middle
when these adjustments
have run their course

10:23.466 --> 10:26.436 align:middle
when reserves are durably
at a level that enables us

10:26.436 --> 10:29.176 align:middle
to control the federal funds
rate using our administered

10:29.176 --> 10:32.126 align:middle
rates, the interest on
reserves and reverse repo,

10:32.636 --> 10:35.686 align:middle
without the need for frequent
use of open market operations.

10:36.206 --> 10:37.486 align:middle
Based on current projections,

10:37.486 --> 10:40.436 align:middle
we expect that the bill
purchases will durably bring the

10:40.436 --> 10:43.146 align:middle
underlying level of reserves
to the ample level sometime

10:43.146 --> 10:45.246 align:middle
in the second quarter
of this year.

10:45.656 --> 10:47.276 align:middle
And when we see that
we've reached that level,

10:47.276 --> 10:50.396 align:middle
we'll begin to gradually reduce
our asset purchases to the level

10:50.396 --> 10:53.866 align:middle
of the underlying trend growth
of demand for our liabilities.

10:54.626 --> 10:58.356 align:middle
As our bill purchases bring the
underlying level of reserves

10:58.356 --> 11:00.316 align:middle
up to an ample level
on a sustained basis,

11:00.746 --> 11:02.446 align:middle
the necessary quantity
of overnight

11:02.566 --> 11:04.556 align:middle
and term repo will
gradually decline.

11:04.556 --> 11:07.296 align:middle
We've already begun the gradual
reduction in the quantity

11:07.296 --> 11:09.326 align:middle
of repo, and we'll continue

11:09.326 --> 11:12.996 align:middle
to reduce those offering amounts
gradually as conditions permit.

11:13.306 --> 11:15.416 align:middle
At some point, we'll also
raise the minimum bid rate.

11:16.116 --> 11:18.816 align:middle
Even after we reach an ample
level of reserves, it's possible

11:18.816 --> 11:21.816 align:middle
that repo operations might
play a role as a backstop

11:21.816 --> 11:24.246 align:middle
and support effective control
of the federal funds rate.

11:25.096 --> 11:27.196 align:middle
And we'll continue to discuss
that issue and review it

11:27.196 --> 11:29.696 align:middle
under our implementation
framework.

11:29.846 --> 11:30.676 align:middle
Coming to your question,

11:31.256 --> 11:33.546 align:middle
in terms of the actually
desired reserve level,

11:33.606 --> 11:36.626 align:middle
we know that reserves will
continue to move up and down

11:36.626 --> 11:39.896 align:middle
over the course of the calendar
year in a wide range depending

11:39.896 --> 11:42.146 align:middle
on volatility in
nonreserve liabilities,

11:42.216 --> 11:45.856 align:middle
particularly the Treasury
General Account, or TGA.

11:46.406 --> 11:49.026 align:middle
In particular, reserve
levels will need to be

11:49.506 --> 11:52.516 align:middle
at a level high enough
to remain ample even

11:52.516 --> 11:54.886 align:middle
when the TGA peaks during
the April tax season.

11:55.776 --> 11:58.776 align:middle
Effectively, what that means
is that we need reserves

11:58.926 --> 12:01.146 align:middle
at all times to be no
lower than they were

12:01.146 --> 12:04.666 align:middle
in early September-and, I
would say, around $1½ trillion,

12:05.096 --> 12:07.596 align:middle
subject to learning more.

12:08.556 --> 12:10.746 align:middle
Reserves are going to move in
a broad range, as I mentioned,

12:10.746 --> 12:12.206 align:middle
and we want to be clear,

12:12.206 --> 12:14.126 align:middle
that will be the
bottom end of the range.

12:14.126 --> 12:15.986 align:middle
We want $1½ trillion
or thereabouts

12:15.986 --> 12:17.236 align:middle
to be the bottom
end of the range.

12:17.236 --> 12:19.936 align:middle
So, most of the time,
reserves will be moving

12:19.936 --> 12:21.996 align:middle
in a range substantially
higher than that

12:22.026 --> 12:24.876 align:middle
but not going below
$1½ trillion.

12:24.976 --> 12:26.146 align:middle
So it's not an-it's
not something

12:26.146 --> 12:27.446 align:middle
that we're aiming
at all the time.

12:27.446 --> 12:29.096 align:middle
We know that reserves
will fluctuate

12:29.436 --> 12:31.686 align:middle
and be substantially higher
than that most of the time.

12:32.306 --> 12:35.346 align:middle
We also want interest on excess
reserves and the fed funds rate

12:35.376 --> 12:38.036 align:middle
to be well within the
FOMC's target range.

12:38.036 --> 12:39.156 align:middle
And we think that'll
be the case now

12:39.156 --> 12:40.486 align:middle
that we made our
technical adjustment.

12:41.466 --> 12:43.076 align:middle
Last points-we're committed

12:43.076 --> 12:45.086 align:middle
to making this adjustment
process a smooth one.

12:45.616 --> 12:48.216 align:middle
We'll provide more details as
we go, and we expect to learn

12:48.216 --> 12:49.466 align:middle
as we go, as we always have.

12:50.136 --> 12:52.986 align:middle
And we're prepared to adjust the
details of the plan as necessary

12:52.986 --> 12:53.906 align:middle
to foster efficient

12:53.906 --> 12:55.926 align:middle
and effective monetary
policy implementation.

12:56.346 --> 12:58.736 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

13:00.826 --> 13:02.036 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

13:02.036 --> 13:03.496 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, thank
you for the question.

13:04.026 --> 13:07.886 align:middle
Following up on Nick's question,
I know you said it's not QE,

13:07.886 --> 13:12.386 align:middle
but it's $390 billion over
five months, which is a lot

13:12.526 --> 13:13.996 align:middle
to expand the balance sheet.

13:14.496 --> 13:16.616 align:middle
I guess I'd ask one more time

13:16.616 --> 13:18.986 align:middle
on Nick's question-is there a
number that you have in mind?

13:19.686 --> 13:22.886 align:middle
Secondly, a lot of people in
the market are sort of concerned

13:22.886 --> 13:24.966 align:middle
that it looks like QE, and
they're trading that way.

13:24.966 --> 13:27.536 align:middle
Are you concerned that the
market is embracing this

13:27.536 --> 13:29.576 align:middle
like a QE program,
and that the rise

13:29.576 --> 13:31.016 align:middle
in the stock market
is linked to it,

13:31.016 --> 13:33.046 align:middle
and you may then
experience something of a

13:33.096 --> 13:35.866 align:middle
"taper tantrum" the way
Chairman Bernanke did

13:35.866 --> 13:36.776 align:middle
when he tried to roll it off?

13:37.166 --> 13:37.886 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:38.316 --> 13:42.916 align:middle
So I'll just-I'll repeat that-we
think that the-we think we need

13:43.276 --> 13:48.196 align:middle
to continue purchases until
the-until reserves are

13:48.196 --> 13:51.956 align:middle
at a level at which they will
not go below $1½ trillion,

13:51.956 --> 13:55.146 align:middle
roughly, during the course
of the calendar year.

13:55.146 --> 13:57.906 align:middle
And we know that the TGA
will move up and down there.

13:57.906 --> 13:59.996 align:middle
So they'll be much higher
than that some of the time,

13:59.996 --> 14:01.616 align:middle
but that's kind of the number,
and we think we'll reach

14:01.616 --> 14:03.446 align:middle
that sometime in
the second quarter.

14:04.126 --> 14:05.266 align:middle
That's our estimate.

14:05.386 --> 14:06.776 align:middle
But we will-we'll know
it when we get there.

14:06.776 --> 14:07.976 align:middle
We'll know it because
we'll be able

14:07.976 --> 14:09.516 align:middle
to be-to control the
federal funds rate

14:09.516 --> 14:12.446 align:middle
without use of-without
active use,

14:12.446 --> 14:15.196 align:middle
ongoing use of open
market operations.

14:15.886 --> 14:18.556 align:middle
You know, our intention

14:18.976 --> 14:23.186 align:middle
of these-for these adjustments
is just to raise the level

14:23.186 --> 14:26.156 align:middle
of reserves and to allow us
to conduct monetary policy

14:26.156 --> 14:27.476 align:middle
in an efficient and
effective manner.

14:27.916 --> 14:29.956 align:middle
And that's-that is
our sole intention.

14:30.586 --> 14:33.596 align:middle
I pointed out on other
occasions, more than once,

14:33.596 --> 14:36.136 align:middle
the differences-the
really specific differences

14:36.136 --> 14:39.526 align:middle
between this and the large-scale
asset purchase programs.

14:40.066 --> 14:40.786 align:middle
We've been over that.

14:41.186 --> 14:43.376 align:middle
In terms of what affects
markets, you know,

14:43.376 --> 14:45.996 align:middle
I think many things
affect markets.

14:45.996 --> 14:48.026 align:middle
It's very hard to say
with any precision

14:48.026 --> 14:49.646 align:middle
at any time what is
affecting markets.

14:49.646 --> 14:53.316 align:middle
What I can tell you is that
you know what our intention is.

14:53.316 --> 14:55.646 align:middle
It is to return reserves
to an ample level.

14:55.686 --> 14:58.056 align:middle
We expect that to happen
during the second quarter.

14:58.546 --> 15:01.766 align:middle
And our plan as we do that
is, as those purchases get

15:01.766 --> 15:04.656 align:middle
to that level, we believe we
can gradually reduce them,

15:04.916 --> 15:09.376 align:middle
and we believe we can also
gradually reduce repo as-as we

15:09.476 --> 15:12.956 align:middle
reach an ample level, as we're
satisfying demand now more

15:12.956 --> 15:14.436 align:middle
from underlying reserves

15:14.436 --> 15:16.746 align:middle
from bill purchases
rather than from repo.

15:17.286 --> 15:19.716 align:middle
And, again, the last thing
I'll say is, we're prepared

15:19.716 --> 15:21.326 align:middle
to adjust the details
of this plan

15:21.326 --> 15:24.596 align:middle
as we've shown ourselves willing
to do depending on conditions.

15:25.596 --> 15:26.226 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

15:28.106 --> 15:30.176 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

15:30.636 --> 15:32.516 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek from
the New York Times.

15:33.046 --> 15:35.486 align:middle
I was hoping that you
could talk a little bit

15:35.486 --> 15:36.736 align:middle
about the labor market.

15:36.736 --> 15:39.486 align:middle
We've recently seen wages
moderating a little bit

15:39.526 --> 15:42.196 align:middle
by some measures, maybe
even declining a little bit

15:42.766 --> 15:43.196 align:middle
by others.

15:43.196 --> 15:46.986 align:middle
As you mentioned, at the lower
end of the sort of talent pool,

15:46.986 --> 15:49.256 align:middle
it seems like we are
seeing those wages climb

15:49.256 --> 15:51.956 align:middle
up a little bit, but it
doesn't seem to be consistent

15:51.956 --> 15:54.066 align:middle
across the entire
sort of average.

15:54.066 --> 15:55.886 align:middle
I was wondering if
you'd talk a little bit

15:55.886 --> 15:58.396 align:middle
about whether you guys
are noticing any cracks,

15:58.396 --> 15:59.546 align:middle
or whether you're
worried about that,

15:59.546 --> 16:00.506 align:middle
or how you're thinking about it.

16:00.506 --> 16:01.696 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:01.756 --> 16:05.516 align:middle
The labor market continues
to-to perform well.

16:05.986 --> 16:08.986 align:middle
The labor market
continues to be strong.

16:09.046 --> 16:11.116 align:middle
We see strong job creation.

16:11.786 --> 16:13.256 align:middle
We see low unemployment.

16:13.966 --> 16:17.056 align:middle
Very importantly, we see labor
force participation continuing

16:17.056 --> 16:19.566 align:middle
to move up, really,
against expectations.

16:19.566 --> 16:22.306 align:middle
If you go back a few years,
you will not find a lot

16:22.306 --> 16:24.626 align:middle
of forecasts suggesting
that we could have been

16:24.626 --> 16:27.566 align:middle
at 63.2 percent overall
labor force participation

16:27.946 --> 16:31.136 align:middle
or the levels of
employment to population

16:31.136 --> 16:33.056 align:middle
that we're seeing now, let
alone the unemployment level.

16:33.056 --> 16:35.916 align:middle
So I think we've learned
quite a lot of good things

16:35.916 --> 16:38.086 align:middle
about the labor market,
good things suggesting

16:38.146 --> 16:40.516 align:middle
that there's been
more room to run.

16:40.516 --> 16:41.996 align:middle
The performance of
wages, I think,

16:41.996 --> 16:43.726 align:middle
has to be seen in that context.

16:43.726 --> 16:47.256 align:middle
We saw-if you go back four
or five years, the four

16:47.256 --> 16:51.426 align:middle
or five major wage statistics
that we track were running

16:51.426 --> 16:53.016 align:middle
at around 2 percent,
and now they're running

16:53.016 --> 16:57.076 align:middle
at around 3 percent,
which is theoretically

16:57.076 --> 16:59.146 align:middle
about where they might
be at full employment.

16:59.266 --> 17:04.476 align:middle
It would consist of inflation
plus-plus productivity growth.

17:04.866 --> 17:08.666 align:middle
The-it's a bit surprising
that, with sustained levels

17:08.776 --> 17:10.836 align:middle
of historically low
unemployment,

17:10.836 --> 17:14.756 align:middle
we haven't seen wages moving
up above that level as we have

17:14.756 --> 17:17.526 align:middle
in other long expansions

17:17.526 --> 17:19.366 align:middle
and other periods
of low unemployment.

17:19.366 --> 17:22.556 align:middle
So you asked, what can
be explaining that?

17:22.556 --> 17:24.466 align:middle
You know, one thing can
be that the natural rate

17:24.466 --> 17:27.526 align:middle
of unemployment is still
lower than we think,

17:27.716 --> 17:31.276 align:middle
that the labor market is not as
tight as it would appear just

17:31.276 --> 17:33.706 align:middle
from the 3½ percent number.

17:34.056 --> 17:37.086 align:middle
And the other can be, I
think, that-as I mentioned,

17:37.536 --> 17:41.066 align:middle
the sort of supply-side shock
or surprise that we're receiving

17:41.066 --> 17:42.956 align:middle
from higher labor
force participation.

17:43.406 --> 17:44.886 align:middle
People are coming in
to the labor market

17:45.276 --> 17:46.766 align:middle
and providing more labor supply,

17:46.766 --> 17:48.666 align:middle
and that is-that's
a great thing.

17:48.666 --> 17:49.716 align:middle
That's a very healthy thing.

17:49.716 --> 17:52.736 align:middle
We're a country that has low
labor force participation

17:52.736 --> 17:58.446 align:middle
compared to essentially all
of our advanced-economy peers,

17:58.626 --> 18:00.876 align:middle
and it's a very good,
positive thing.

18:00.876 --> 18:02.896 align:middle
Nonetheless, it represents
more labor supply,

18:02.896 --> 18:04.416 align:middle
and it may be holding
down wages.

18:04.826 --> 18:07.216 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

18:10.626 --> 18:12.976 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

18:14.176 --> 18:17.146 align:middle
In terms of the framework
review and a little bit

18:17.146 --> 18:21.376 align:middle
on Chris's question earlier,
there is a general feeling now

18:21.976 --> 18:23.846 align:middle
in the markets and
among analysts

18:23.846 --> 18:26.986 align:middle
that you're basically
setting us up for some form

18:26.986 --> 18:29.166 align:middle
of inflation-target averaging

18:29.166 --> 18:33.006 align:middle
where you let the inflation rate
run above the 2 percent target

18:33.006 --> 18:35.736 align:middle
for some time to
make up for the time

18:35.736 --> 18:37.076 align:middle
that it has spent below that.

18:37.366 --> 18:39.696 align:middle
Is that a fair or
reasonable assessment

18:39.696 --> 18:41.306 align:middle
of where you think
you're going to end up?

18:41.766 --> 18:46.116 align:middle
What would you, Chairman Powell,
think of that idea personally?

18:46.116 --> 18:49.686 align:middle
And, as long as I'm asking,
do you have any more details

18:49.686 --> 18:51.496 align:middle
on when we can expect the
results of the review?

18:52.736 --> 18:53.376 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:53.506 --> 18:57.356 align:middle
Thanks. I'll just say that
we undertook the review

18:57.436 --> 18:59.966 align:middle
because we felt, and I
felt, that it was time

18:59.966 --> 19:04.436 align:middle
to incorporate the realities
of what we could call the

19:04.436 --> 19:06.586 align:middle
"new normal" into
our policy framework.

19:07.636 --> 19:11.356 align:middle
And some aspects of that new
normal would include ongoing

19:11.786 --> 19:15.186 align:middle
powerful global disinflationary
trends, which have led

19:15.186 --> 19:19.806 align:middle
to lower-than-target inflation
many places in the world;

19:20.186 --> 19:23.656 align:middle
secondly, a flat Phillips curve,
by which I mean low levels

19:23.656 --> 19:25.166 align:middle
of sensitivity of inflation

19:25.166 --> 19:29.816 align:middle
to resource utilization-for
example, low unemployment; and,

19:29.816 --> 19:33.606 align:middle
thirdly, a much lower neutral
real interest rate here

19:33.606 --> 19:34.286 align:middle
and around the world.

19:34.926 --> 19:38.086 align:middle
So those are challenging
conditions for monetary policy

19:38.086 --> 19:41.506 align:middle
to deliver on our statutory
goals of maximum employment

19:41.506 --> 19:44.186 align:middle
and stable prices,
although I would say that,

19:44.186 --> 19:47.816 align:middle
under our existing framework,
we've been able to succeed

19:47.816 --> 19:50.986 align:middle
or get close to succeeding it
for most of the time lately

19:51.576 --> 19:53.466 align:middle
to achieve those goals,
although we do struggle,

19:53.656 --> 19:55.736 align:middle
as other central banks do,
with the inflation goal.

19:56.396 --> 19:58.476 align:middle
So this is about reviewing
our strategy, tools,

19:58.476 --> 20:00.846 align:middle
and communications to
assure that they're the best

20:00.846 --> 20:02.456 align:middle
that we can do to
achieve those goals

20:02.456 --> 20:04.576 align:middle
in this environment
on a sustained basis.

20:05.296 --> 20:07.516 align:middle
And we continued our
discussions at this meeting.

20:08.006 --> 20:11.626 align:middle
I'm very, very pleased
at the process so far.

20:12.066 --> 20:14.806 align:middle
It's included the 14 Fed Listens
events around the country,

20:14.806 --> 20:18.616 align:middle
at which we've engaged with a
full range of people and groups

20:18.616 --> 20:20.006 align:middle
across American society.

20:20.006 --> 20:23.056 align:middle
That was a very, very
positive experience,

20:23.056 --> 20:23.986 align:middle
and I think we learned a lot.

20:24.086 --> 20:26.986 align:middle
We've now had a series of-a
number of FOMC meetings

20:26.986 --> 20:28.276 align:middle
at which we've reviewed
what we've learned

20:28.276 --> 20:31.806 align:middle
and also dug deeply
into strategy,

20:31.806 --> 20:33.006 align:middle
tools, and communications.

20:33.546 --> 20:35.476 align:middle
I expect that we will
conclude the review

20:35.476 --> 20:37.866 align:middle
and announce our conclusions
around the middle of the year.

20:39.076 --> 20:42.266 align:middle
Right now, we are just at
the point of coming together

20:42.266 --> 20:43.926 align:middle
to put all that together.

20:44.446 --> 20:46.276 align:middle
So I think I'm not the person

20:46.276 --> 20:48.816 align:middle
who should be telling you my
personal preferences right now.

20:49.376 --> 20:52.356 align:middle
I'm trying to-and we were trying
to come together as a group

20:52.356 --> 20:53.426 align:middle
around a set of answers.

20:53.936 --> 20:56.896 align:middle
I feel very positive
about-that we're going to come

20:57.016 --> 21:01.396 align:middle
up with some good results, and
I'm just going to have to wait

21:01.396 --> 21:03.656 align:middle
until we get to that
point to announce them.

21:04.696 --> 21:06.886 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

21:08.016 --> 21:09.866 align:middle
Donna Borak with CNN.

21:09.866 --> 21:12.026 align:middle
Going back to your
outlook for global growth,

21:12.156 --> 21:13.926 align:middle
we've seen some significant
headwinds,

21:13.926 --> 21:14.786 align:middle
as you mentioned earlier,

21:14.786 --> 21:17.936 align:middle
with the easing-with the
partial U.S.-China trade deal,

21:18.306 --> 21:20.856 align:middle
but now that there's some new
concern following the outbreak

21:20.856 --> 21:23.916 align:middle
of the coronavirus-that it
might shake global growth.

21:24.066 --> 21:26.386 align:middle
We're already seeing
reports from Ford and Toyota

21:26.386 --> 21:28.036 align:middle
that they're planning to shut

21:28.086 --> 21:29.996 align:middle
down their assembly
plants for an extra week.

21:30.436 --> 21:32.506 align:middle
Apple is rerouting
their supply chains.

21:32.596 --> 21:36.106 align:middle
Starbucks is shutting down
close to thousands of stores.

21:36.456 --> 21:39.286 align:middle
Are you worried at all about
what the impact would be

21:39.286 --> 21:41.656 align:middle
on the U.S. economy,
and do you see

21:41.656 --> 21:44.486 align:middle
that as a significant
risk-excuse me-to the outlook

21:44.486 --> 21:45.066 align:middle
at this point?

21:45.206 --> 21:45.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:45.506 --> 21:47.626 align:middle
So let me talk about the
coronavirus specifically,

21:47.626 --> 21:50.426 align:middle
and then I'll turn more to
global growth more generally.

21:50.706 --> 21:52.546 align:middle
First, it's a-it's a
very serious issue,

21:52.546 --> 21:55.126 align:middle
and I want to start by
acknowledging the significant

21:55.126 --> 21:56.576 align:middle
and considerable human suffering

21:57.096 --> 21:58.616 align:middle
that the virus is
already causing.

21:59.116 --> 22:02.396 align:middle
There is likely to be some
disruption to activity in China

22:02.396 --> 22:05.286 align:middle
and possibly globally based on
the spread of the virus to date

22:05.286 --> 22:07.516 align:middle
and the travel restrictions
and business closures

22:07.566 --> 22:09.066 align:middle
that have already been imposed.

22:09.826 --> 22:13.646 align:middle
Of course, the situation is
really in its early stages,

22:13.646 --> 22:16.346 align:middle
and it's very uncertain
about how far it will spread

22:16.346 --> 22:18.616 align:middle
and what the macroeconomic
effects will be in China

22:18.616 --> 22:20.776 align:middle
and its immediate
trading partners

22:20.776 --> 22:22.386 align:middle
and neighbors and
around the world.

22:22.856 --> 22:25.466 align:middle
So, in light of that
uncertainty, I'm not going

22:25.466 --> 22:27.226 align:middle
to speculate about
it at this point.

22:27.226 --> 22:29.036 align:middle
I will just tell
you that, of course,

22:29.106 --> 22:32.586 align:middle
we are very carefully
monitoring the situation.

22:33.246 --> 22:37.886 align:middle
And, you know, as you suggested,
our framework ultimately is,

22:38.226 --> 22:39.816 align:middle
what are the potential
ramifications

22:39.816 --> 22:43.406 align:middle
for the U.S. economy and for the
achievement of our dual mandate?

22:43.576 --> 22:47.496 align:middle
More broadly, though, if I can
talk about the global economy

22:47.496 --> 22:50.716 align:middle
for a second-and if you look
at the backdrop, you know,

22:51.386 --> 22:54.446 align:middle
if you go back to 2017, that was
the year of synchronized growth

22:54.746 --> 22:59.186 align:middle
that lasted into the middle of
2018, and then you saw slowing

22:59.186 --> 23:01.456 align:middle
in growth, which lasted right
through the end of last year.

23:01.456 --> 23:04.316 align:middle
The fourth-quarter growth
globally was quite weak last

23:04.316 --> 23:06.666 align:middle
year, and a number of
factors played into that.

23:06.666 --> 23:07.946 align:middle
It wasn't any one factor.

23:08.026 --> 23:11.926 align:middle
There was trade policy
uncertainty, absolutely,

23:11.926 --> 23:14.676 align:middle
but also there was the decision
by the Chinese authorities

23:14.676 --> 23:17.376 align:middle
to try to rein in leverage
and financial excesses.

23:17.816 --> 23:19.166 align:middle
There was a turn-downturn

23:19.246 --> 23:21.346 align:middle
in the global high-tech
manufacturing cycle

23:21.836 --> 23:23.216 align:middle
in global auto production.

23:23.716 --> 23:29.606 align:middle
There were some idiosyncratic
strains in some countries

23:29.606 --> 23:31.206 align:middle
like Argentina, Turkey.

23:31.206 --> 23:33.016 align:middle
And then, later, in
Hong Kong and Chile,

23:33.016 --> 23:34.516 align:middle
you had social unrest.

23:35.086 --> 23:38.646 align:middle
So, all of those things
were playing into that cycle

23:38.646 --> 23:42.926 align:middle
of weakening growth
over the course of 2019

23:42.926 --> 23:44.176 align:middle
and the last half of '18.

23:44.656 --> 23:47.386 align:middle
I would say, now there are
grounds for what I would call

23:47.496 --> 23:50.166 align:middle
"cautious optimism"
about the outlook now

23:50.166 --> 23:51.036 align:middle
for the global economy.

23:51.236 --> 23:54.976 align:middle
Many analysts are predicting
a pickup in growth this year,

23:54.976 --> 23:58.816 align:middle
although still to relatively
modest growth rates.

23:59.006 --> 24:01.646 align:middle
And I would-people are pointing
to, and we would point to,

24:01.646 --> 24:04.906 align:middle
supportive financial conditions,
the easing of trade tensions,

24:04.906 --> 24:06.876 align:middle
the lower odds of a hard Brexit.

24:07.366 --> 24:10.186 align:middle
The high-tech manufacturing
industry does appear

24:10.186 --> 24:13.766 align:middle
to be rebounding well in
Asia, including in China.

24:14.366 --> 24:17.536 align:middle
The latest
indicators-manufacturing PMIs,

24:17.536 --> 24:19.266 align:middle
for example-suggest

24:19.266 --> 24:20.936 align:middle
that manufacturing
may have bottomed out,

24:21.106 --> 24:23.376 align:middle
that they're still below
50 in many jurisdictions,

24:23.376 --> 24:26.046 align:middle
but they have moved
up off of their lows.

24:26.156 --> 24:28.596 align:middle
I would-I would just say,
none of this is assured.

24:29.186 --> 24:32.346 align:middle
As I mentioned, we saw the
fourth quarter of last year came

24:32.346 --> 24:34.056 align:middle
in quite weak-weaker
than expected.

24:34.486 --> 24:36.576 align:middle
We are not at all assured
of a global rebound,

24:36.576 --> 24:39.136 align:middle
but there are signs and
reasons to expect it.

24:39.176 --> 24:43.216 align:middle
And then comes the coronavirus,
which, again, it's too early

24:43.216 --> 24:45.276 align:middle
to say what the effects will be.

24:45.276 --> 24:46.956 align:middle
Of course, we're,
as I mentioned,

24:46.956 --> 24:48.076 align:middle
monitoring it carefully.

24:48.646 --> 24:50.496 align:middle
There will clearly
be implications,

24:50.496 --> 24:53.996 align:middle
at least in the near term,
for Chinese output and,

24:53.996 --> 24:56.786 align:middle
I would guess, for some of
their close-in neighbors,

24:56.786 --> 24:59.146 align:middle
and we'll just have to see
what the effect is globally.

25:00.266 --> 25:03.236 align:middle
BRENDAN GREELEY.

25:03.936 --> 25:05.826 align:middle
Brendan Greeley with
the Financial Times.

25:06.386 --> 25:12.106 align:middle
As recently as 2018, interest on
excess reserves was at the top

25:12.106 --> 25:14.586 align:middle
of the band, sort of dragging
the fed funds rate up.

25:15.176 --> 25:19.446 align:middle
Over the course of the
adjustments since last year,

25:19.696 --> 25:22.306 align:middle
it's moved steadily down, closer
to the bottom of the band.

25:22.566 --> 25:26.996 align:middle
So, if you're moving it back up,
how high do you want to get it?

25:26.996 --> 25:30.086 align:middle
Are we looking to get interest
on excess reserves at the top

25:30.086 --> 25:33.476 align:middle
of the band again as
the fed funds rate moves

25:33.476 --> 25:34.386 align:middle
to the middle of the band?

25:34.386 --> 25:38.816 align:middle
And should we see movements
in IOER as an indicator

25:38.816 --> 25:40.746 align:middle
that we're approaching "ample"?

25:41.656 --> 25:42.246 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:42.246 --> 25:45.116 align:middle
So our stated goal
is to keep IOER

25:45.116 --> 25:47.316 align:middle
and the federal funds rate
well within the range.

25:47.966 --> 25:48.336 align:middle
That's it.

25:48.566 --> 25:49.366 align:middle
Well within the range.

25:49.846 --> 25:52.466 align:middle
And that, clearly, you know-5
basis points from the bottom

25:52.466 --> 25:54.656 align:middle
or from the top isn't that.

25:55.236 --> 25:56.476 align:middle
So that's why we moved back up.

25:56.476 --> 25:56.946 align:middle
You're right.

25:56.946 --> 26:00.316 align:middle
Last year we were-we
saw some tightness

26:00.316 --> 26:02.006 align:middle
as reserves were draining
out of the system.

26:02.006 --> 26:04.376 align:middle
We saw the-gradually moving up.

26:05.106 --> 26:07.226 align:middle
And, in hindsight, we
know what was happening.

26:08.226 --> 26:11.896 align:middle
With ample reserves, we
see-we see that it's possible

26:11.896 --> 26:15.056 align:middle
to bring the interest
on reserves rate

26:15.056 --> 26:16.506 align:middle
up to 10 basis points.

26:16.506 --> 26:18.936 align:middle
So now we're-now we are well
within the range, I would say.

26:19.196 --> 26:19.556 align:middle
BRENDAN GREELEY.

26:19.556 --> 26:22.726 align:middle
We are. So you don't think
that, as the fed funds goes back

26:22.726 --> 26:23.816 align:middle
up towards the center
of the range,

26:23.856 --> 26:24.966 align:middle
that IOER is going
to end up at the top?

26:24.996 --> 26:25.356 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:27.346 --> 26:27.886 align:middle
Don't know that.

26:27.886 --> 26:29.316 align:middle
We want it to be well
within the range.

26:29.316 --> 26:30.836 align:middle
I think we'll continue
to adjust it

26:30.836 --> 26:32.116 align:middle
to the extent it's appropriate.

26:32.526 --> 26:33.616 align:middle
Ultimately, what we're trying

26:33.616 --> 26:35.726 align:middle
to do is deliver a federal
funds rate that's well

26:35.726 --> 26:36.496 align:middle
within the range.

26:37.086 --> 26:38.786 align:middle
IOER is just a tool to do that.

26:39.246 --> 26:41.486 align:middle
If we-if we need to make
changes to keep it well

26:41.486 --> 26:42.646 align:middle
within the range, we'll do that.

26:43.096 --> 26:45.526 align:middle
It just means-I mean, the thing
that matters for the economy is

26:45.526 --> 26:49.376 align:middle
that we keep the federal funds
rate-which is the rate that,

26:49.376 --> 26:51.906 align:middle
of course, transmits into
other money market rates,

26:51.906 --> 26:54.906 align:middle
which ultimately
transmit into all kinds

26:54.906 --> 26:56.766 align:middle
of financial conditions-that's
what we care about.

26:57.216 --> 26:59.906 align:middle
So, ultimately, we
will-we will use that tool

26:59.906 --> 27:01.206 align:middle
to keep it well within
the range.

27:02.556 --> 27:06.426 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

27:06.426 --> 27:06.686 align:middle
Thank you.

27:06.786 --> 27:08.076 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

27:08.406 --> 27:10.466 align:middle
Just to sort of close
the loop on one thing,

27:10.696 --> 27:14.336 align:middle
where do discussions stand
on a standing repo facility?

27:14.436 --> 27:16.946 align:middle
Because what you've said
so far sort of implies

27:17.016 --> 27:20.976 align:middle
that there won't be one or
that it will be a limited one.

27:21.506 --> 27:22.716 align:middle
So if you could just, you know,

27:22.756 --> 27:24.156 align:middle
let us know where that
discussion stands.

27:24.156 --> 27:25.716 align:middle
And also- UNIDENTIFIED SPEAKER.

27:25.716 --> 27:26.186 align:middle
Can you turn mic over?

27:26.326 --> 27:26.806 align:middle
Turn the mic over.

27:26.806 --> 27:27.106 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

27:27.186 --> 27:27.616 align:middle
Sorry about that.

27:27.616 --> 27:28.156 align:middle
Sorry about that.

27:28.156 --> 27:28.916 align:middle
Did you get the question?

27:30.226 --> 27:31.696 align:middle
Did you hear the question okay?

27:31.696 --> 27:32.016 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:32.016 --> 27:34.896 align:middle
I did. I don't think-I'm not
sure your colleagues did.

27:34.896 --> 27:35.446 align:middle
[Laughter] HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

27:35.446 --> 27:39.286 align:middle
Where do the discussions
stand on standing repo?

27:39.526 --> 27:40.156 align:middle
[Aside] That was great.

27:40.156 --> 27:41.236 align:middle
And the other thing
is, on the, you know,

27:41.236 --> 27:42.476 align:middle
on the standing repo facility

27:42.476 --> 27:45.586 align:middle
and on the purchases-Treasury
bill purchases,

27:46.456 --> 27:49.656 align:middle
as you enter this new regime
later in the second quarter

27:49.656 --> 27:53.416 align:middle
or later in the year, will that
be preannounced by amounts?

27:53.416 --> 27:55.306 align:middle
Will there be preset amounts
like you're doing now,

27:55.306 --> 27:58.036 align:middle
or will that just be something
that people will have to sort

27:58.036 --> 28:00.056 align:middle
of intuit and figure
out as you go along?

28:00.116 --> 28:00.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:00.516 --> 28:01.506 align:middle
So I'll go in reverse order.

28:01.506 --> 28:05.686 align:middle
When we-when we make decisions
about the steps we're going

28:05.686 --> 28:08.656 align:middle
to be taking in that adjustment
process that I described

28:08.656 --> 28:10.976 align:middle
at some length, we'll be
making them as early as we can

28:10.976 --> 28:13.696 align:middle
and as transparently as
we can-and specifically.

28:13.746 --> 28:14.816 align:middle
But we're not at that stage.

28:14.816 --> 28:17.346 align:middle
It's January, and we're several
months away from-from that.

28:18.006 --> 28:20.636 align:middle
In terms of the standing repo
facility, as I mentioned in,

28:20.636 --> 28:24.986 align:middle
I guess, in my answer
to an earlier question,

28:25.366 --> 28:30.726 align:middle
there may well be a role
for repo in this system even

28:30.726 --> 28:33.786 align:middle
after we're at an
ample-reserves level.

28:34.406 --> 28:36.706 align:middle
Now, we haven't decided
what that role is,

28:36.706 --> 28:38.876 align:middle
we have not decided-we
haven't at all made a decision

28:38.876 --> 28:39.966 align:middle
on a standing repo facility.

28:39.966 --> 28:40.396 align:middle
We haven't.

28:40.396 --> 28:42.736 align:middle
We've had a couple of
discussions about it,

28:42.736 --> 28:44.796 align:middle
as you know, back in, I
guess, June and October.

28:45.426 --> 28:46.776 align:middle
And there's a range of views.

28:46.776 --> 28:48.256 align:middle
We're going to return,
I would say,

28:48.256 --> 28:49.666 align:middle
fairly soon to this question.

28:50.326 --> 28:53.026 align:middle
And I think it was wise to wait,

28:53.026 --> 28:55.796 align:middle
because we're still getting a
better sense of what that-what

28:55.796 --> 28:56.736 align:middle
that world looks like.

28:57.186 --> 28:59.266 align:middle
And, you know, it's really
going to be a question of,

28:59.266 --> 29:00.356 align:middle
how useful will it be?

29:00.356 --> 29:01.886 align:middle
What will be the
costs and benefits?

29:01.886 --> 29:06.036 align:middle
And my thinking is, we will
return to that fairly soon,

29:06.036 --> 29:08.696 align:middle
and I wouldn't assume a
decision one way or the other.

29:08.696 --> 29:10.816 align:middle
Really, we haven't made one,
because we haven't had to.

29:10.816 --> 29:12.586 align:middle
And I think you-when you delay,

29:12.586 --> 29:14.156 align:middle
you get more information
by waiting.

29:14.156 --> 29:14.926 align:middle
It's good to wait.

29:15.286 --> 29:16.986 align:middle
We don't have any urgency
in making that decision,

29:16.986 --> 29:19.856 align:middle
because we're still
trying to find that sort

29:19.856 --> 29:21.966 align:middle
of stable equilibrium
where we're

29:21.966 --> 29:24.006 align:middle
in an ample-reserves regime.

29:26.056 --> 29:28.606 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

29:30.096 --> 29:32.586 align:middle
Heather Long from
the Washington Post.

29:33.156 --> 29:35.526 align:middle
I'm wondering, do you think

29:35.526 --> 29:38.486 align:middle
that there is a financial
stability risk

29:38.556 --> 29:40.086 align:middle
from climate change?

29:40.636 --> 29:42.216 align:middle
You've spoken several times

29:42.216 --> 29:45.836 align:middle
that you think severe weather
events are happening more often

29:45.836 --> 29:49.706 align:middle
and that the Fed is monitoring
what that could physically do

29:49.706 --> 29:51.966 align:middle
to a bank or a financial
institution,

29:51.966 --> 29:53.786 align:middle
but that's sort of
one institution.

29:53.786 --> 29:56.216 align:middle
Do you think there's
a systemwide risk

29:56.796 --> 30:00.086 align:middle
that may-that could
develop from climate change?

30:00.286 --> 30:03.006 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:03.856 --> 30:06.346 align:middle
So that's an interesting
question-the question being,

30:06.756 --> 30:10.166 align:middle
is there a systemwide
financial stability risk?

30:10.246 --> 30:14.116 align:middle
I'd say, over-over the longer
term, it's certainly possible,

30:14.776 --> 30:17.206 align:middle
and I would say that
sort of feeds

30:17.206 --> 30:18.366 align:middle
into the way we're thinking

30:18.556 --> 30:20.876 align:middle
about climate change
as an institution.

30:20.876 --> 30:26.016 align:middle
So, as I've mentioned, climate
change is an important issue,

30:26.016 --> 30:28.276 align:middle
a very important issue, but
it's essentially assigned

30:28.276 --> 30:31.096 align:middle
to many other agencies
in the federal government

30:31.096 --> 30:36.386 align:middle
and state governments
for leadership on that.

30:36.386 --> 30:39.586 align:middle
And, importantly,
society's overall response

30:39.586 --> 30:42.306 align:middle
to climate change
needs to be decided

30:42.416 --> 30:45.176 align:middle
by elected officials
and not by the Fed.

30:45.856 --> 30:48.146 align:middle
All of that said, if
you look at our mandate,

30:48.446 --> 30:50.376 align:middle
we've got a monetary
policy mandate and,

30:50.796 --> 30:53.046 align:middle
more immediately perhaps,
a supervisory mandate

30:53.046 --> 30:55.856 align:middle
where we're supervising
financial markets, utilities,

30:55.856 --> 30:57.726 align:middle
and financial institutions,
banks.

30:58.276 --> 31:00.786 align:middle
And we haven't-we share
overall responsibility

31:00.786 --> 31:02.856 align:middle
for financial stability
with a number of agencies.

31:03.236 --> 31:06.916 align:middle
And that latter part-I think the
public has every right to expect

31:06.916 --> 31:09.266 align:middle
and will expect that
we will assure

31:09.266 --> 31:12.916 align:middle
that the financial system
is resilient and robust

31:12.916 --> 31:14.426 align:middle
against the risks
from climate change.

31:14.426 --> 31:16.586 align:middle
Now, I think that's
got to be right.

31:16.746 --> 31:19.586 align:middle
We are in the very early stages,
as are other central banks,

31:19.586 --> 31:21.156 align:middle
in understanding
just what that means.

31:21.566 --> 31:24.366 align:middle
And there's quite a lot of
work going on around the world

31:24.366 --> 31:26.326 align:middle
at other central banks
and at the Fed, too,

31:26.326 --> 31:27.216 align:middle
to think that through.

31:27.966 --> 31:31.326 align:middle
But I do think it's-in that
sense, it has to be part

31:31.326 --> 31:37.366 align:middle
of our role, and-but
not the overall response

31:37.366 --> 31:38.786 align:middle
of society to climate change.

31:38.786 --> 31:39.376 align:middle
That's not us.

31:39.536 --> 31:39.886 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

31:40.276 --> 31:41.456 align:middle
And if I could follow up,

31:41.456 --> 31:45.376 align:middle
can you clarify why the Fed
hasn't signed on to the Network

31:45.416 --> 31:47.396 align:middle
for Greening the
Financial System

31:47.396 --> 31:49.356 align:middle
like 40 other central
banks have?

31:49.456 --> 31:49.766 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:49.986 --> 31:53.786 align:middle
So we've attended all of their
meetings and taken part in them,

31:54.336 --> 31:58.606 align:middle
and we've been looking at
joining in one form or another

31:58.606 --> 31:59.676 align:middle
and talking to them about that.

31:59.676 --> 32:01.326 align:middle
We probably will do
that at some point.

32:02.006 --> 32:03.566 align:middle
So that's-that's an
ongoing question.

32:03.566 --> 32:05.846 align:middle
But we're very much
attending those meetings

32:05.846 --> 32:09.776 align:middle
and taking part in them.

32:09.986 --> 32:10.696 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

32:11.216 --> 32:12.296 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

32:12.376 --> 32:13.866 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business Network.

32:14.206 --> 32:16.136 align:middle
So there was a signing
of a ratified USMCA

32:16.136 --> 32:17.116 align:middle
at the White House today.

32:17.296 --> 32:20.736 align:middle
Mid-February, the phase-one
China deal gets-goes

32:20.736 --> 32:21.326 align:middle
into effect.

32:21.596 --> 32:24.046 align:middle
Have you seen business
investment pick up at all?

32:25.016 --> 32:28.906 align:middle
If not, what will it take to get
that business investment going

32:28.906 --> 32:31.476 align:middle
as this uncertainty
is-is being cleared up?

32:32.496 --> 32:33.166 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:33.626 --> 32:38.946 align:middle
Yes. So I guess I'd start
by saying that the fact

32:38.946 --> 32:41.966 align:middle
that we've reached a phase-one
deal with China and the fact

32:41.966 --> 32:44.466 align:middle
that we've moved ahead closer

32:44.466 --> 32:50.496 align:middle
to getting USM[C]A agreed-those
are potentially positive things

32:50.496 --> 32:51.686 align:middle
for the economy without
question.

32:51.766 --> 32:52.366 align:middle
Without question.

32:53.156 --> 32:55.076 align:middle
And financial markets-the
reaction

32:55.076 --> 32:57.416 align:middle
of financial markets is very
consistent with that perception.

32:58.066 --> 33:00.466 align:middle
A sustained reduction
in uncertainty

33:00.466 --> 33:03.506 align:middle
over time should improve
business sentiment, investment,

33:04.046 --> 33:05.866 align:middle
which would provide
some additional support

33:05.866 --> 33:07.296 align:middle
for the economy.

33:08.476 --> 33:11.256 align:middle
It's important, though, to bear
in mind a couple of things.

33:11.946 --> 33:15.836 align:middle
First, trade policy
uncertainty remains elevated.

33:16.466 --> 33:19.626 align:middle
Businesses continue to
identify it as an ongoing risk.

33:20.006 --> 33:24.796 align:middle
We still have two or even
three active trade discussions

33:24.796 --> 33:27.306 align:middle
that are going on in the
public square right now.

33:27.786 --> 33:31.616 align:middle
So it hasn't gone away, and
we've just come through a round

33:32.166 --> 33:36.236 align:middle
of talking to our vast
network of comments-sorry,

33:36.356 --> 33:37.526 align:middle
contacts in the business world.

33:38.116 --> 33:41.116 align:middle
And I think, clearly,
these are seen

33:41.116 --> 33:43.786 align:middle
as positive developments going
forward, but there's a bit

33:43.786 --> 33:46.326 align:middle
of a wait-and-see attitude-is
this going to be sustained?

33:46.846 --> 33:49.336 align:middle
The agreements have to
be implemented, too,

33:49.336 --> 33:50.736 align:middle
and that'll take
quite some time.

33:50.736 --> 33:53.936 align:middle
So, I would say, we need
to be a little bit patient

33:53.936 --> 33:56.456 align:middle
about the effect on the economy.

33:56.996 --> 33:59.596 align:middle
There's also the global economy.

33:59.646 --> 34:04.226 align:middle
You could-you could well see
manufacturing-as I mentioned,

34:04.226 --> 34:06.736 align:middle
manufacturing PMIs
have started to tick

34:06.736 --> 34:09.646 align:middle
up consistently among
both advanced economies

34:09.646 --> 34:10.896 align:middle
and emerging market economies.

34:11.136 --> 34:15.276 align:middle
We do not see a decisive
recovery, but it's possible

34:15.276 --> 34:17.136 align:middle
that this mix of
positive developments

34:17.136 --> 34:20.696 align:middle
and also accommodative
financial conditions could spur

34:20.696 --> 34:22.006 align:middle
further growth.

34:23.126 --> 34:23.996 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

34:26.196 --> 34:27.706 align:middle
Hi, Mike Derby with Dow Jones.

34:28.206 --> 34:32.826 align:middle
Do you have any greater sense
of what-what was going wrong

34:32.826 --> 34:34.566 align:middle
with the repo market
starting in September?

34:34.566 --> 34:35.076 align:middle
There seemed to be,

34:35.076 --> 34:37.686 align:middle
you know-whether it was a
one-off event, you know,

34:37.686 --> 34:39.836 align:middle
too tight around tax
payments and debt settlement,

34:39.836 --> 34:42.226 align:middle
or whether there's a
more enduring issue going

34:42.226 --> 34:44.916 align:middle
on with the market,
more structural forces

34:45.006 --> 34:46.856 align:middle
that are basically
gumming up the repo sector.

34:46.856 --> 34:47.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:47.746 --> 34:51.946 align:middle
Yes. As for those forces,
so, as we indicated,

34:51.946 --> 34:55.576 align:middle
we would undertake a, you know,
serious review of that question

34:55.576 --> 34:59.826 align:middle
and look at both our regulations
and also supervisory practices.

34:59.826 --> 35:02.676 align:middle
And we'd be prepared
to adjust those in ways

35:02.676 --> 35:06.896 align:middle
that might encourage
liquidity to flow more easily

35:06.896 --> 35:08.876 align:middle
in the system as long

35:08.876 --> 35:10.576 align:middle
as it didn't undermine
safety and soundness.

35:10.976 --> 35:12.096 align:middle
And so we've undertaken that,

35:12.096 --> 35:13.536 align:middle
and we've done a ton
of work, actually.

35:13.536 --> 35:14.916 align:middle
We don't have anything
to announce here today,

35:14.916 --> 35:17.246 align:middle
but I feel good about
the-what we've learned there.

35:17.726 --> 35:20.066 align:middle
I think-you mentioned
other factors.

35:20.336 --> 35:23.206 align:middle
We will be announcing
our findings.

35:23.546 --> 35:25.946 align:middle
I'm not going to give you a
time, but we're-we're well along

35:25.946 --> 35:27.096 align:middle
in that assessment at this time.

35:27.316 --> 35:31.426 align:middle
I think we also found out,
though, that the level

35:31.426 --> 35:36.986 align:middle
of reserves that we need in the
system to conduct our operations

35:36.986 --> 35:39.946 align:middle
without frequent resort to open
market operations was higher

35:39.946 --> 35:41.446 align:middle
than we had thought
and was higher

35:41.446 --> 35:42.396 align:middle
than others had thought, too.

35:42.916 --> 35:45.496 align:middle
We learned that we can't let
reserves-we shouldn't let

35:45.496 --> 35:47.866 align:middle
reserves go below
$1½ trillion-roughly,

35:48.206 --> 35:50.896 align:middle
the level of early
September-at any point.

35:50.936 --> 35:54.276 align:middle
And that means that reserves
will move through in a range

35:54.276 --> 35:56.896 align:middle
over the course of the year that
will be substantially higher

35:56.896 --> 35:59.126 align:middle
than $1½ [trillion], but
they won't ever go below.

35:59.126 --> 36:01.296 align:middle
So $1½ [trillion] is
not a target level.

36:01.296 --> 36:02.876 align:middle
It is the bottom of a range

36:03.546 --> 36:05.426 align:middle
in which reserves will
be expected to move.

36:05.856 --> 36:07.626 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

36:09.846 --> 36:13.336 align:middle
Hi, Mr. Chairman.

36:13.336 --> 36:18.306 align:middle
I just wanted to ask about how
your balance sheet operations

36:18.306 --> 36:21.896 align:middle
and repo-temporary repos-how
they affect the system

36:21.896 --> 36:23.026 align:middle
and who they're helping.

36:23.446 --> 36:26.386 align:middle
Clearly, they're mostly
designed, as you said,

36:26.386 --> 36:29.656 align:middle
to get-keep the fed funds rate
in the range you're looking for.

36:29.656 --> 36:33.166 align:middle
But, certainly, the repo
market is also used heavily

36:33.166 --> 36:35.486 align:middle
by hedge funds and other
Wall Street institutions.

36:35.486 --> 36:38.786 align:middle
And so, how would you
explain to, sort of,

36:38.786 --> 36:42.416 align:middle
Main Street why you're doing
all this for that market?

36:42.486 --> 36:44.856 align:middle
And how would you
address criticism

36:44.856 --> 36:47.156 align:middle
that it is helping
mostly Wall Street,

36:47.156 --> 36:48.496 align:middle
along with everything else?

36:48.496 --> 36:48.806 align:middle
Thank you.

36:48.956 --> 36:49.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:49.456 --> 36:52.336 align:middle
Well, let me just stress
that we have a very specific

36:52.336 --> 36:54.516 align:middle
and important reason for caring

36:54.516 --> 36:57.086 align:middle
about money market operations
generally, and that just is

36:57.116 --> 37:00.796 align:middle
that we are-our monetary policy
decisions are transmitted

37:00.796 --> 37:02.436 align:middle
through the financial
markets-through the money

37:02.436 --> 37:04.266 align:middle
markets into other
financial markets

37:04.266 --> 37:05.636 align:middle
and into broader
financial conditions.

37:05.636 --> 37:09.206 align:middle
So we care that money markets
are operating smoothly.

37:09.206 --> 37:11.546 align:middle
And they stopped
operating smoothly,

37:11.546 --> 37:13.036 align:middle
briefly, back in September.

37:13.036 --> 37:14.786 align:middle
And so we acted.

37:14.786 --> 37:18.226 align:middle
This is a one-time thing that
we're doing to adjust the level

37:18.226 --> 37:20.616 align:middle
of reserves so that the
money markets will be able

37:20.616 --> 37:22.436 align:middle
to operate smoothly
on an ongoing basis.

37:22.776 --> 37:25.996 align:middle
Repo markets are
important, because that's

37:25.996 --> 37:30.346 align:middle
where Treasury securities-the
purchase of Treasury securities,

37:30.866 --> 37:32.786 align:middle
which is the way-you
know, the federal-part

37:32.786 --> 37:34.516 align:middle
of the way the federal
government is funding

37:34.516 --> 37:36.136 align:middle
its operations.

37:36.136 --> 37:37.616 align:middle
It's the way those are financed.

37:37.736 --> 37:41.036 align:middle
So these are largely
Treasury securities

37:41.596 --> 37:42.646 align:middle
that have been purchased

37:42.726 --> 37:45.856 align:middle
by dealers to-for
distribution to-and buyers,

37:45.856 --> 37:46.826 align:middle
to a substantial extent.

37:46.826 --> 37:49.076 align:middle
That's what's going on in
the Treasury repo market.

37:49.526 --> 37:51.486 align:middle
So it's just the
financing for-for that.

37:51.486 --> 37:55.086 align:middle
Now, again, we don't-that
activity is a-is a

37:55.086 --> 37:56.186 align:middle
market activity.

37:56.956 --> 38:01.026 align:middle
We're not looking to eliminate
volatility or protect anybody

38:01.026 --> 38:01.896 align:middle
from volatility at all.

38:01.896 --> 38:04.286 align:middle
What we care about
is that volatility

38:04.286 --> 38:07.726 align:middle
in the repo market can affect
the transmission of-of our

38:07.726 --> 38:09.776 align:middle
policy decisions to
federal funds rate,

38:10.146 --> 38:14.256 align:middle
and that really is
important for the public.

38:14.356 --> 38:16.446 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

38:16.446 --> 38:18.746 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

38:19.306 --> 38:22.616 align:middle
Chair Powell, is the Fed going
to vote tomorrow on changes

38:22.616 --> 38:25.616 align:middle
to the Volcker rule
restrictions on banks investing

38:25.686 --> 38:27.146 align:middle
in venture capital funds?

38:27.606 --> 38:30.176 align:middle
What can you tell us about
what the Fed is considering?

38:30.676 --> 38:32.476 align:middle
And why make those changes?

38:32.476 --> 38:33.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:34.016 --> 38:37.696 align:middle
Sure. So we will be
looking tomorrow and voting

38:37.696 --> 38:44.076 align:middle
on a-a new part of-part of
the existing Volcker rule,

38:44.076 --> 38:46.396 align:middle
and that is the covered
fund provisions of the rule.

38:46.396 --> 38:48.736 align:middle
And we'll be making a
bunch of proposed revisions

38:48.796 --> 38:52.066 align:middle
that we believe are
faithful to both the letter

38:52.066 --> 38:53.106 align:middle
and the spirit of the law.

38:53.106 --> 38:54.276 align:middle
We're going to put
those proposals

38:54.276 --> 38:57.936 align:middle
out for public discussion, and
we're going to listen carefully,

38:57.936 --> 39:00.626 align:middle
as we always do, to public
comments on those proposals.

39:00.926 --> 39:03.766 align:middle
Again, we believe that they
will be-and you'll see them

39:03.766 --> 39:05.536 align:middle
tomorrow-will be
public-you know,

39:05.536 --> 39:08.416 align:middle
will be publicly-we have a Board
meeting tomorrow to do that.

39:08.416 --> 39:09.436 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

39:09.436 --> 39:11.036 align:middle
Now, is this just the
venture capital funds,

39:11.036 --> 39:13.726 align:middle
or are also you changing
the rules for hedge funds

39:13.726 --> 39:14.626 align:middle
and private equity funds?

39:14.626 --> 39:14.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:14.836 --> 39:15.896 align:middle
It's covered funds.

39:16.596 --> 39:19.366 align:middle
It's-so it's that covered-so
it's not the proprietary trading

39:19.366 --> 39:20.056 align:middle
part of Volcker.

39:20.056 --> 39:21.286 align:middle
It's the covered funds part.

39:21.286 --> 39:22.076 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

39:22.076 --> 39:25.526 align:middle
And is there a chance that
banks could take this and run

39:25.526 --> 39:29.566 align:middle
and maybe even get involved in
mortgage-backed securities again

39:30.516 --> 39:31.576 align:middle
and risky investments?

39:32.046 --> 39:32.596 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:32.816 --> 39:34.706 align:middle
You know, we'll-we think

39:34.706 --> 39:37.846 align:middle
that what we're doing is-is
very consistent with safety

39:37.846 --> 39:40.196 align:middle
and soundness and absolutely
consistent with the letter

39:40.196 --> 39:41.446 align:middle
and spirit of the Volcker rule.

39:41.446 --> 39:43.406 align:middle
We'll be-we'll be getting
comments from people,

39:43.406 --> 39:45.486 align:middle
and we'll be looking into that
question, among many others.

39:46.656 --> 39:47.716 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

39:51.516 --> 39:51.906 align:middle
Thank you.

39:51.986 --> 39:52.936 align:middle
Thank you, Chairman Powell.

39:53.646 --> 39:57.116 align:middle
I'd like to turn your attention
back to China and the health

39:57.116 --> 39:59.046 align:middle
of its financial
sector in particular.

39:59.586 --> 40:01.206 align:middle
And I guess I'm basing
my question

40:01.376 --> 40:05.006 align:middle
on reading the transcripts
from 2014 that came

40:05.006 --> 40:06.196 align:middle
out earlier this month.

40:06.736 --> 40:09.526 align:middle
Just-in March of that
year, there was concern

40:09.526 --> 40:12.176 align:middle
about the Chinese economy,
and one of your colleagues

40:12.176 --> 40:14.826 align:middle
on the FOMC at that
time asked the staff

40:15.056 --> 40:18.776 align:middle
about how the Chinese economy
would hurt-hit the U.S. economy.

40:19.506 --> 40:22.696 align:middle
And the staffer said that there
was a-what they were worried

40:22.696 --> 40:26.096 align:middle
about was that not only was the
Chinese economy slowing down,

40:26.676 --> 40:28.186 align:middle
but there was the
financial sector.

40:28.286 --> 40:30.726 align:middle
And the quote was:
"There's a tremendous amount

40:31.186 --> 40:33.386 align:middle
of dodgy loans in China."

40:34.556 --> 40:36.726 align:middle
Now, I was wondering if
you could give us an update

40:36.866 --> 40:39.776 align:middle
on these problems in the
Chinese financial sector.

40:39.776 --> 40:41.806 align:middle
And how do you think it
might impact the economy,

40:41.806 --> 40:44.206 align:middle
particularly now
that they've been hit

40:44.206 --> 40:45.396 align:middle
by this unexpected shock?

40:46.326 --> 40:47.156 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:48.036 --> 40:52.496 align:middle
Well, China has had a
problem for some years,

40:53.126 --> 40:59.636 align:middle
including from that period-2014
up until, I guess, '17 or '18,

40:59.636 --> 41:02.626 align:middle
and I thought-which was
essentially just a lot of debt

41:02.666 --> 41:04.086 align:middle
for an emerging market.

41:04.086 --> 41:06.236 align:middle
For an economy at the-at
the stage of evolution

41:06.236 --> 41:08.946 align:middle
of the Chinese economy,
they had very high levels

41:08.946 --> 41:12.446 align:middle
of debt-not sovereign debt
the way we think of it,

41:12.996 --> 41:16.776 align:middle
but more business debt-debt
of state-owned enterprises

41:16.776 --> 41:18.166 align:middle
and also just private
businesses.

41:18.856 --> 41:23.086 align:middle
And a couple of years ago the
authorities decided to try

41:23.086 --> 41:24.846 align:middle
to get that under
control-to stop the growth

41:24.846 --> 41:25.976 align:middle
and to control it.

41:25.976 --> 41:26.926 align:middle
And that-as I mentioned,

41:26.926 --> 41:30.476 align:middle
that's one of the reasons
why Chinese growth slowed,

41:30.476 --> 41:32.696 align:middle
and it's one of the reasons
why global growth slowed,

41:32.696 --> 41:34.036 align:middle
because we felt that.

41:34.456 --> 41:36.716 align:middle
And they've actually stuck

41:36.786 --> 41:39.316 align:middle
to that even during
this difficult period

41:39.316 --> 41:42.026 align:middle
when they were experiencing,
you know,

41:42.026 --> 41:44.796 align:middle
strains from trade negotiations
and that kind of thing.

41:44.796 --> 41:47.186 align:middle
The authorities have stuck
to that and have, again,

41:47.186 --> 41:48.756 align:middle
continued to try to do that,

41:49.426 --> 41:52.046 align:middle
so-to try to control
the growth of debt.

41:52.046 --> 41:54.936 align:middle
And that's important
that they do that.

41:55.926 --> 42:01.456 align:middle
We don't think that-that
there's any imminent risk there,

42:01.456 --> 42:02.986 align:middle
although you-as you point out,

42:02.986 --> 42:04.946 align:middle
the coronavirus thing
is a significant thing,

42:04.946 --> 42:07.556 align:middle
which will-will have some
effects on the Chinese economy,

42:07.556 --> 42:08.576 align:middle
at least in the short term.

42:08.576 --> 42:10.906 align:middle
The Chinese economy
is very important

42:10.906 --> 42:12.056 align:middle
in the global economy now.

42:12.636 --> 42:17.726 align:middle
And, you know, we-when
China's economy slows down,

42:17.726 --> 42:20.816 align:middle
we do feel that-not as
much as countries, though,

42:20.816 --> 42:23.296 align:middle
that are near China or that
trade more actively with China,

42:23.296 --> 42:24.886 align:middle
like some of the Western
European countries.

42:25.416 --> 42:26.446 align:middle
We still have-you know,

42:26.446 --> 42:29.576 align:middle
85 percent of our
economy is domestic.

42:29.576 --> 42:33.026 align:middle
And we have a much smaller
external sector, trade sector,

42:33.026 --> 42:33.836 align:middle
than other economies just

42:33.836 --> 42:35.146 align:middle
because of our physical
location.

42:35.526 --> 42:37.526 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

42:37.716 --> 42:43.966 align:middle
Hi. Brian Cheung
here, Yahoo Finance.

42:43.966 --> 42:45.926 align:middle
So the combination
of T bill purchases

42:45.926 --> 42:48.626 align:middle
and repo operations have
been described colloquially

42:48.626 --> 42:49.456 align:middle
as "liquidity."

42:49.826 --> 42:52.566 align:middle
I'm just wondering if
you semantically agree

42:52.566 --> 42:54.616 align:middle
with that description-then,
secondly, what the impact

42:54.706 --> 42:56.226 align:middle
of that has been on risk assets.

42:56.226 --> 42:58.896 align:middle
I don't know if that's something
that, you know, the Board

42:58.896 --> 43:02.026 align:middle
or Reserve Banks are formally
looking into or just looking

43:02.026 --> 43:03.296 align:middle
at what the effect
of it has been.

43:03.586 --> 43:05.176 align:middle
How are you kind of thinking
about that going forward?

43:05.496 --> 43:06.436 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

43:06.746 --> 43:07.946 align:middle
Well, so, two questions.

43:07.946 --> 43:09.386 align:middle
I mean, in terms of liquidity,

43:09.386 --> 43:11.516 align:middle
I think what we're doing is
what I said: We're trying

43:11.516 --> 43:14.596 align:middle
to raise the level of
reserves back up to a level

43:14.596 --> 43:17.046 align:middle
so that banks can meet
their reserve requirements,

43:17.046 --> 43:20.416 align:middle
and that there's enough
reserves in the system

43:20.416 --> 43:24.056 align:middle
that we don't see reserve
scarcity, and we don't have

43:24.176 --> 43:27.706 align:middle
to use repo operations to
provide additional reserves.

43:27.706 --> 43:31.136 align:middle
So I think, as I mentioned, we
believe we can get to that state

43:31.786 --> 43:34.296 align:middle
at the current pace sometime
in the second quarter.

43:34.686 --> 43:37.456 align:middle
In terms of effects on risk
assets, as I said earlier,

43:37.456 --> 43:41.976 align:middle
it's very hard to say what is
affecting financial markets

43:41.976 --> 43:44.236 align:middle
with any precision or
confidence at a given time.

43:44.476 --> 43:47.146 align:middle
It's not our intention to change
the stance of monetary policy.

43:47.336 --> 43:50.436 align:middle
These were designed to provide
more reserves and, really,

43:50.436 --> 43:53.526 align:middle
to do that in order to
enable better transmission

43:53.526 --> 43:58.896 align:middle
of our-of our rate
decisions into the economy

43:58.896 --> 44:00.196 align:middle
under our chosen framework.

44:00.276 --> 44:03.066 align:middle
That's really the purpose
of what we're doing.

44:03.256 --> 44:04.126 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

44:07.766 --> 44:09.116 align:middle
Hi. Jean Yung with MNI.

44:09.116 --> 44:14.046 align:middle
I wanted to ask about the
framework review again.

44:15.066 --> 44:17.776 align:middle
Would a shift of
focus to inflation

44:17.776 --> 44:20.246 align:middle
over an average period-would
that call

44:20.246 --> 44:23.896 align:middle
for a different policy stance
if you made that shift?

44:24.256 --> 44:27.536 align:middle
And whether or not we know
the answer to that question,

44:27.986 --> 44:31.316 align:middle
would the Fed consider changing
the stance of monetary policy

44:31.506 --> 44:34.606 align:middle
for that reason even
if there was no change

44:34.606 --> 44:35.836 align:middle
to the economic outlook?

44:36.066 --> 44:37.236 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:37.236 --> 44:38.426 align:middle
Well, as you know,
we're comfortable

44:38.426 --> 44:39.796 align:middle
with our current policy stance.

44:39.796 --> 44:40.666 align:middle
We think it's appropriate.

44:40.666 --> 44:42.246 align:middle
We think it'll remain
appropriate as long

44:42.246 --> 44:45.906 align:middle
as data coming in are broadly
in keeping with our outlook.

44:46.476 --> 44:50.366 align:middle
Over time-over time,
though-let me take a step back.

44:50.656 --> 44:53.356 align:middle
Over time, an average
inflation-targeting framework

44:53.356 --> 44:56.616 align:middle
would be different than our
current framework, in the sense

44:57.136 --> 45:01.956 align:middle
that it wouldn't be a-there
would be some aspect of trying

45:01.956 --> 45:04.986 align:middle
to make inflation average 2
percent over time, which means,

45:04.986 --> 45:08.406 align:middle
if it runs below 2 percent
for a time, it has to run

45:08.406 --> 45:09.946 align:middle
above to bring the average up.

45:09.946 --> 45:11.376 align:middle
So that is a different
framework.

45:11.376 --> 45:12.566 align:middle
Our current framework is one

45:12.566 --> 45:18.256 align:middle
where we say-or we'd be equally
concerned with deviations

45:18.256 --> 45:20.736 align:middle
of inflation from
target on either side.

45:21.296 --> 45:23.866 align:middle
But that isn't-that
doesn't suggest an intention

45:23.866 --> 45:28.406 align:middle
specifically to have those
deviations be symmetric.

45:28.896 --> 45:30.276 align:middle
In other words, that
would-consistent

45:30.276 --> 45:33.966 align:middle
with that would be having all
the deviations be on one side,

45:33.966 --> 45:35.286 align:middle
which is what we've
had, actually.

45:35.676 --> 45:37.476 align:middle
So I think it is a
change in framework,

45:37.476 --> 45:40.866 align:middle
and over time it would lead to
a different approach to policy.

45:41.146 --> 45:43.336 align:middle
Again, I'm not trying
to-I don't want to comment

45:43.336 --> 45:44.596 align:middle
on the current stance of policy,

45:44.596 --> 45:45.866 align:middle
which we do think
is appropriate.

45:46.856 --> 45:52.026 align:middle
DON LEE. Don Lee
with the L.A. Times.

45:52.026 --> 45:54.116 align:middle
I wanted to ask you
about the stock market.

45:55.026 --> 45:57.136 align:middle
By historical comparisons,
as you know,

45:57.366 --> 46:00.216 align:middle
valuations are considerably
high.

46:00.246 --> 46:03.976 align:middle
And I just wonder how much
discussion and concern you

46:04.036 --> 46:06.016 align:middle
and your colleagues
have about that

46:06.016 --> 46:07.636 align:middle
and what risks you
see for the economy.

46:08.326 --> 46:09.006 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:09.236 --> 46:10.846 align:middle
So we-we look at
a very broad range

46:10.846 --> 46:12.946 align:middle
of financial conditions
where-there isn't any one

46:12.946 --> 46:15.506 align:middle
financial condition
that we-that we look at.

46:15.706 --> 46:18.946 align:middle
And when we look at financial
conditions, what matters

46:18.946 --> 46:23.786 align:middle
for the real economy is
substantial changes in-or,

46:23.786 --> 46:26.656 align:middle
material changes in financial
conditions that are sustained

46:26.656 --> 46:28.746 align:middle
over a period of time.

46:29.316 --> 46:31.746 align:middle
If I can, maybe I'll-maybe
I'll answer that in the context

46:31.746 --> 46:34.046 align:middle
of our overall financial
stability framework.

46:34.046 --> 46:35.026 align:middle
That's one way to look at it.

46:35.366 --> 46:37.116 align:middle
So we look at-when we look
at financial stability,

46:37.116 --> 46:39.426 align:middle
we look at-really, we've
got four pillars to that,

46:39.796 --> 46:42.806 align:middle
the first of which is leverage
in the financial system that is

46:42.946 --> 46:44.726 align:middle
at a comfortable level.

46:44.726 --> 46:46.376 align:middle
Our banks, particularly
our large banks,

46:46.376 --> 46:47.516 align:middle
have high levels of capital.

46:48.286 --> 46:50.856 align:middle
The second is leverage in
the nonfinancial sector,

46:50.856 --> 46:52.946 align:middle
and that divides into
households and businesses.

46:53.336 --> 46:56.936 align:middle
Households' debt to
GDP is-has been coming

46:56.936 --> 46:58.556 align:middle
down since the financial crisis.

46:58.556 --> 46:59.506 align:middle
It's not moving up.

46:59.506 --> 47:01.226 align:middle
It's at low-low levels compared

47:01.226 --> 47:02.296 align:middle
to what it was before
the crisis.

47:02.296 --> 47:06.836 align:middle
So not every household-but, in
the aggregate, household debt is

47:07.146 --> 47:08.536 align:middle
in a good place,
a very good place.

47:08.906 --> 47:10.106 align:middle
Business debt has
been moving up.

47:10.106 --> 47:12.116 align:middle
We've been calling
that out for more

47:12.116 --> 47:14.396 align:middle
than a year-substantially
more than a year,

47:14.396 --> 47:15.736 align:middle
and it's something
we're focused on.

47:15.736 --> 47:17.866 align:middle
And we've taken appropriate
measures

47:17.866 --> 47:19.026 align:middle
and are monitoring carefully.

47:19.026 --> 47:20.166 align:middle
But we think it's not something

47:20.166 --> 47:21.496 align:middle
that would threaten
financial stability,

47:21.496 --> 47:22.696 align:middle
but more be an amplifier.

47:23.386 --> 47:24.826 align:middle
The other one is
asset purchases,

47:24.826 --> 47:26.416 align:middle
getting to your-sorry,
asset prices,

47:26.416 --> 47:27.126 align:middle
getting to your question.

47:27.596 --> 47:30.316 align:middle
We do see asset valuations

47:30.316 --> 47:33.826 align:middle
as being somewhat elevated-I
do-somewhat elevated.

47:33.826 --> 47:35.566 align:middle
If you look at risk
spreads, they're narrow.

47:35.566 --> 47:37.336 align:middle
If you look at P/Es,
they're high.

47:37.716 --> 47:41.256 align:middle
I think the way to think about-a
good way-one way to think

47:41.256 --> 47:42.476 align:middle
about equity prices, though, is,

47:42.476 --> 47:46.096 align:middle
what's the premium
you're getting paid

47:46.096 --> 47:48.326 align:middle
to own equities rather
than risk-free debt?

47:48.706 --> 47:50.506 align:middle
And that's also at
fairly low levels,

47:50.506 --> 47:51.806 align:middle
but not extremely low levels.

47:51.806 --> 47:54.416 align:middle
So valuations are high,
but not at extremes.

47:54.476 --> 47:56.986 align:middle
The final factor
is funding risk.

47:56.986 --> 48:00.236 align:middle
Are big financial
institutions and other players

48:00.236 --> 48:02.896 align:middle
in the financial system
funded with stable funding,

48:02.896 --> 48:04.046 align:middle
or is there a lot of run risk?

48:04.046 --> 48:07.166 align:middle
And the answer is, very stable
funding, for the most part.

48:07.166 --> 48:10.886 align:middle
So if you look at, overall-what
you see, in my view, is,

48:11.766 --> 48:12.676 align:middle
vulnerabilities

48:12.676 --> 48:15.966 align:middle
to the financial-to financial
stability are moderate overall.

48:17.926 --> 48:23.386 align:middle
KATY O'DONNELL.

48:23.496 --> 48:25.246 align:middle
Hi. Katy O'Donnell, Politico.

48:25.246 --> 48:29.046 align:middle
I wanted to know, do you support
Governor Brainard's vision

48:29.046 --> 48:31.016 align:middle
for the Community
Reinvestment Act reform?

48:31.076 --> 48:33.606 align:middle
And is this something that
we could see the Fed formally

48:33.606 --> 48:34.686 align:middle
propose at some point?

48:35.186 --> 48:35.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:35.916 --> 48:39.666 align:middle
So let me say that I
think this is a good time

48:39.666 --> 48:46.116 align:middle
to do-to update CRA, really,
in a way that is a win-win both

48:46.116 --> 48:47.726 align:middle
for the intended
beneficiaries-low-

48:47.726 --> 48:49.986 align:middle
and moderate-income
communities-and also for banks

48:50.056 --> 48:53.756 align:middle
that would like to have more
certainty about-about what does

48:53.756 --> 48:56.486 align:middle
and doesn't qualify
and that sort of thing.

48:56.486 --> 48:58.466 align:middle
The law can both
be more effective

48:58.576 --> 49:00.426 align:middle
and more efficient
is-it comes down to.

49:00.426 --> 49:02.026 align:middle
And we think this is a good way.

49:02.026 --> 49:03.696 align:middle
It's also just a
good time to take

49:03.696 --> 49:08.316 align:middle
on board the way the delivery
of banking services has changed

49:08.316 --> 49:10.576 align:middle
through technology and
demographic change as well.

49:11.506 --> 49:14.466 align:middle
We worked very hard to try
to get on the same page

49:14.466 --> 49:15.906 align:middle
with the other two agencies.

49:16.406 --> 49:21.666 align:middle
We think that an interagency
final rule together would be the

49:21.666 --> 49:22.356 align:middle
best outcome.

49:22.436 --> 49:25.196 align:middle
We're sorry we haven't been able
to get there, and we still hold

49:25.196 --> 49:28.026 align:middle
out some hope that
we will be able to.

49:28.466 --> 49:31.866 align:middle
We spent a lot of time
on research and analysis

49:32.006 --> 49:34.156 align:middle
and looking at meaningful
reform.

49:34.156 --> 49:37.046 align:middle
You saw Governor Brainard's
recent speech presenting some

49:37.046 --> 49:38.406 align:middle
of the thinking and
the analysis.

49:38.406 --> 49:40.206 align:middle
And we haven't made any
decisions about what we're going

49:40.206 --> 49:41.736 align:middle
to do, about whether
we'll propose-I mean,

49:41.736 --> 49:44.786 align:middle
our focus has been
entirely on trying to get

49:44.786 --> 49:47.116 align:middle
to agreement with
the OCC, really.

49:47.666 --> 49:49.986 align:middle
So we haven't made any decisions

49:49.986 --> 49:51.746 align:middle
about what we're going
to do going forward.

49:52.556 --> 49:57.066 align:middle
In terms of, you know-Governor
Brainard led our oversight

49:57.066 --> 49:59.576 align:middle
committee over these activities
for many years, and I asked her

49:59.576 --> 50:01.756 align:middle
to take the lead on
CRA modernization,

50:02.366 --> 50:04.496 align:middle
which is a high priority for us.

50:04.596 --> 50:07.656 align:middle
I was comfortable with her
speech, and I'm comfortable

50:07.656 --> 50:12.166 align:middle
with the work we've done and
with the Fed's position on this.

50:12.166 --> 50:14.816 align:middle
As I said, we haven't chosen
to bring a proposal forward.

50:15.666 --> 50:18.356 align:middle
We haven't decided what to
do, whether-going forward.

50:18.356 --> 50:20.626 align:middle
And we're not going to
comment on the other proposal.

50:20.626 --> 50:21.936 align:middle
It's just not appropriate to do.

50:21.936 --> 50:22.836 align:middle
It's not about us.

50:23.456 --> 50:24.586 align:middle
It's not about our views.

50:24.916 --> 50:27.286 align:middle
It's about the views of
the interested parties.

50:27.846 --> 50:29.456 align:middle
HANNAH LANG.

50:32.066 --> 50:33.416 align:middle
Hannah Lang with
American Banker.

50:33.766 --> 50:34.896 align:middle
Thank you for being here today.

50:34.976 --> 50:38.496 align:middle
I wanted to ask about Vice
Chair Quarles's recent speech

50:38.536 --> 50:42.986 align:middle
on bank supervision in which
he laid out some suggestions

50:43.556 --> 50:47.346 align:middle
for making changes to
the supervisory regime.

50:47.346 --> 50:50.826 align:middle
I wanted to ask, do you
agree with his approach?

50:50.826 --> 50:53.166 align:middle
And are there any
plans to codify some

50:53.166 --> 50:54.716 align:middle
of these suggestions
later in the year?

50:55.446 --> 50:55.686 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:55.776 --> 51:00.616 align:middle
So I do agree that-the
principles that he articulated

51:00.616 --> 51:02.326 align:middle
of firm and fair supervision

51:02.326 --> 51:04.776 align:middle
and effective transparency
in communications.

51:05.116 --> 51:06.786 align:middle
I also think it's a good thing

51:06.856 --> 51:09.696 align:middle
that we would have
brighter lines

51:09.696 --> 51:12.606 align:middle
to define our LISCC
supervisory portfolio,

51:12.706 --> 51:14.096 align:middle
which we haven't
really had to date.

51:14.806 --> 51:18.046 align:middle
Remember that when a firm moves
from one portfolio to another,

51:18.046 --> 51:20.086 align:middle
that doesn't mean that
its level of scrutiny

51:20.086 --> 51:21.216 align:middle
or supervision will change.

51:22.066 --> 51:25.026 align:middle
So-and I also thought
that, you know,

51:25.026 --> 51:29.936 align:middle
the-he's raising some very
interesting questions about,

51:30.516 --> 51:32.756 align:middle
if you-in the first part of
the speech, where he's talking

51:32.756 --> 51:36.536 align:middle
about regulation and
supervision and, you know,

51:36.536 --> 51:41.286 align:middle
how to-how to balance the desire
for transparency and due process

51:41.286 --> 51:43.256 align:middle
in everything the
government does with the needs

51:43.256 --> 51:44.366 align:middle
of confidential supervision.

51:44.826 --> 51:47.236 align:middle
It's a very challenging
question.

51:47.236 --> 51:50.146 align:middle
It's one that could
use further thought.

51:50.456 --> 51:53.666 align:middle
And as far as the specific,
you know, proposals,

51:53.886 --> 51:56.006 align:middle
there's-you know, they're
interesting, you know,

51:56.006 --> 51:58.326 align:middle
and need further development
and will need lots of comment

51:58.326 --> 52:01.406 align:middle
and that sort of thing.

52:02.156 --> 52:06.956 align:middle
MARK HAMRICK.

52:07.846 --> 52:08.356 align:middle
Thank you.

52:10.276 --> 52:11.856 align:middle
Here we go.

52:11.856 --> 52:12.936 align:middle
Sorry, I had it [the
microphone] upside down.

52:12.936 --> 52:13.676 align:middle
That's not too good.

52:14.316 --> 52:16.246 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, Mark
Hamrick with Bankrate.

52:16.246 --> 52:17.156 align:middle
Thank you.

52:17.406 --> 52:20.236 align:middle
I wanted to ask you a
question about the-one

52:20.236 --> 52:21.826 align:middle
of the unintended consequences

52:21.826 --> 52:24.706 align:middle
over the years-long low
interest rate environment,

52:24.706 --> 52:27.456 align:middle
and that is that savers
haven't gotten as much return

52:27.456 --> 52:30.086 align:middle
as would have otherwise
been the case.

52:30.086 --> 52:32.926 align:middle
What would you say to those
individuals who've seen the Fed

52:32.926 --> 52:35.616 align:middle
again cutting rates,
eroding their ability

52:35.616 --> 52:37.536 align:middle
to get a higher return
on their savings?

52:37.536 --> 52:40.216 align:middle
And how much would you take
their plight into consideration?

52:40.516 --> 52:42.026 align:middle
How much can you
sympathize with them?

52:42.026 --> 52:42.236 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

52:42.236 --> 52:46.486 align:middle
Sure. So we-we are
assigned a job by Congress,

52:46.486 --> 52:48.036 align:middle
and that is to use our tools

52:48.066 --> 52:51.516 align:middle
to pursue maximum
employment and stable prices.

52:51.516 --> 52:52.956 align:middle
And that's what-that's
our focus.

52:53.576 --> 52:55.586 align:middle
Now, monetary policy
is a blunt instrument,

52:55.746 --> 52:56.686 align:middle
but it's a powerful one.

52:56.686 --> 52:59.706 align:middle
So I think if you
look-look at the time

52:59.706 --> 53:00.806 align:middle
since the financial crisis.

53:00.806 --> 53:04.006 align:middle
That was-in the week [wake]
of fairly modest growth,

53:04.076 --> 53:06.316 align:middle
it was a powerful recovery
in the labor market.

53:06.316 --> 53:10.246 align:middle
And part of that just is the
effect of lower interest rates.

53:10.336 --> 53:14.836 align:middle
So, if-so many, many people
benefit from low interest rates.

53:14.836 --> 53:16.816 align:middle
In fact, you don't
hear-when you talk to the low-

53:16.816 --> 53:19.606 align:middle
and moderate-income communities,
one thing you don't hear is,

53:19.606 --> 53:20.456 align:middle
"You ought to raise rates."

53:21.136 --> 53:22.026 align:middle
That's not what you hear.

53:22.026 --> 53:23.906 align:middle
You hear quite the
opposite, which is, you know,

53:24.126 --> 53:26.286 align:middle
"Please do whatever you can
to keep this expansion going."

53:26.816 --> 53:29.486 align:middle
I absolutely sympathize
with people.

53:29.486 --> 53:32.256 align:middle
If you're living on just
the interest in a bank

53:33.026 --> 53:36.656 align:middle
or on fixed-income generally,
then that's a challenging thing.

53:36.656 --> 53:37.906 align:middle
On the other hand,
if you own a home,

53:38.316 --> 53:40.376 align:middle
home values-the housing
market has recovered,

53:40.916 --> 53:44.126 align:middle
other financial assets have
recovered, so-but, yes,

53:44.126 --> 53:46.116 align:middle
for some people who are limited

53:46.116 --> 53:48.986 align:middle
to those sources plus
whatever other help they get,

53:49.466 --> 53:50.416 align:middle
it can be challenging.

53:50.526 --> 53:52.226 align:middle
I mean, we have to
do what's best

53:52.226 --> 53:55.596 align:middle
for the-for the overall
society and the economy.

53:56.216 --> 54:03.116 align:middle
That's-those are our
orders from Congress.

54:04.906 --> 54:13.096 align:middle
Thanks very much.

